{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"UPDATE to the pause state above: the in-flight review-plan finished while I was recording,\nand it returned BLOCK, not PASS. So no attestation was signed, `--status` still reports\n`stale-material`, and the push precondition remains UNMET. Still not pushing. ps4 is now\nblocked on a code change, not just a signature refresh.\n\nTHE FINDING IS CORRECT — I verified the citation in source before accepting it (the inverse\nof my earlier mistake, where I dismissed a real finding after checking the wrong tree).\n\nReviewer's claim: my narrowed idempotent check reads stderr only, but the sibling precedent\nit is modelled on matches BOTH streams. Confirmed at\nsrc/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py:739-742, in delete_issue:\n\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc:\n        err_text = (exc.stderr or \"\") + (exc.stdout or \"\")\n        if \"404\" in err_text or \"not found\" in err_text.lower():\n            # Already deleted — idempotent success\n            return {\"status\": \"not_found\", \"key\": jira_key}\n\nMine (`_is_idempotent_link_removal`) only inspects `exc.stderr`.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS, and it is the same class of bug as the one being fixed. `link delete` runs\nWITHOUT `--json` (story 25ae: ACLI rejects that flag there), so its failure output is\nhuman-readable text on the exit-code path, and a \"404 / not found\" message can land on\nSTDOUT rather than stderr. A stderr-only test would then classify a GENUINE concurrent-\nremoval race as a failure: `links_failed` inflates, and under\nREBAR_RECONCILER_FAIL_SILENT_NOOP=1 the canary could fire on a benign race. That is a false\nalarm in the honest-counter direction — less dangerous than the silent-success defect this\nticket fixes, but it would erode trust in the very counter being added, which is the whole\npoint of the change.\n\nTHE FIX (small, one function plus coverage), for the resumption:\n1. `_is_idempotent_link_removal` in dispatch_apply_phases.py takes the CalledProcessError\n   (or both streams) rather than `stderr`, and matches\n   `(exc.stderr or \"\") + (exc.stdout or \"\")`, citing acli.py:739 as the precedent.\n2. Add a case to tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_outbound_link_failure_accounting.py:\n   a delete failing with the 404 marker on STDOUT and empty stderr must count\n   `links_applied=1, links_failed=0`. That oracle fails against the current stderr-only\n   implementation, so it pins the finding rather than restating it.\n3. Keep \"absent from BOTH streams is not idempotent\" — that part of the narrowing stands and\n   is what makes the stderr-less regression test meaningful.\n4. THEN re-run review-plan (expect PASS), verify `--status` is certified, and only then push\n   ps4. Do not touch description/file_impact after the review — that ordering trap already\n   cost two CI cycles.\n\nNote the fix does NOT change the ticket's file_impact: dispatch_apply_phases.py and that test\nfile are both already declared, so step 4 needs no plan edit.\n\nAll local gates remain green on b20afb53b3; this finding is about correctness under a stdout-\ncarried 404, which no current test exercises."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786535626356438001,"uuid":"b9f5ad8e-cfd4-4e93-85eb-62e8df96cec9"}